# Build artifacts. These are anchored to the repository root on purpose: a bare
# `plaesy` also matches any path *component* named plaesy, which silently
# excluded scripts/cmd/plaesy/ — the entire CLI source — from version control.
# The commands existed on disk, passed their tests, and were never committed.
reload.sh
# Root-anchored, because a bare `plaesy` also matches any path *component*
# named plaesy — which silently excluded scripts/cmd/plaesy/, the entire CLI
# source, from version control. The commands existed on disk, passed their tests,
# and were never committed.
/plaesy
/plaesy.exe
/plaesy-test
# A Windows `go build` in the CLI source directory writes plaesy.exe *there*,
# so the extension spelling needs a rule of its own too. Safe unanchored: it can
# only match a file of exactly that name, never the `plaesy/` directory. This
# must NOT be root-anchored — anchored, it silently fails to match
# scripts/plaesy.exe and leaves the binary staged for the next `git add -A`.
plaesy.exe
.clinerules/byterover-rules.md
.kilo*
.roo/rules/byterover-rules.md
.windsurf/rules/byterover-rules.md
.cursor/rules/byterover-rules.mdc
.kiro/steering/byterover-rules.md
.qoder/rules/byterover-rules.md
.augment/rules/byterover-rules.md


# Plaesy framework output dirs, ignored here as well as globally in
# ~/.gitignore_global, so a fresh clone and this checkout agree on what is
# generated rather than source.
#
# ...but the session state and the task system are *not* generated output, and
# the global ignore excludes the whole `.plaesy` directory. Without these
# negations a task file is tracked from the moment it is created and then
# vanishes from `git add -A` the first time `plaesy tasks move` relocates it:
# the old path shows as deleted, the new one is invisible, and the file
# silently leaves version control. That is the tracked-binary bug from 2026-09-28
# in a different costume — an ignore rule, correctly written for a directory
# that is mostly generated, silently classifying the ungenerated minority too.
# A repo .gitignore outranks ~/.gitignore_global, so these win.
#
# The order is load-bearing: git cannot re-include a file whose parent
# directory is excluded, so `!.plaesy/` has to come first and the generated
# subdirectories have to be re-ignored after it. Reversed, the negation is
# silently inert and the bug returns with no error.
!.plaesy/
.plaesy/analysis*
.plaesy/checklists*
.plaesy/instructions*
.plaesy/roles*
.plaesy/scripts*
.plaesy/templates*
# The root `plaesy` binary was committed for the whole life of this repository,
# a 14.9 MB Windows PE blob, even though `/plaesy` has been listed above the
# entire time. Ignore rules do not apply to a file that is already tracked, so
# neither the rule nor the fix to the `clean` target that had introduced it
# (it ran `git add -Af`) could ever have worked. `git rm --cached` is what
# actually removed it, in 2026-09-28.
#
# The lesson is the reason TestNoBuildArtifactIsTracked and
# TestBuildOutputPathsAreIgnored exist: "we wrote the ignore rule" is not the
# same claim as "the ignore rule is doing something".

.claude*
AGENTS.md
CLAUDE.md

# Node
node_modules/

# Go build/coverage artifacts
*.out
cover.out
# Windows/MSYS stray redirect target ("... > nul" creates a real file named nul)
nul
